#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::ostringstream oss;
    oss << markdown;
    return oss.str();
}
```

This function uses the `std::ostringstream` class to convert the markdown text to an HTML string. The `std::ostringstream` object is used to output the markdown text, and the resulting HTML string is returned.

Here's an example usage of the function:

```cpp
int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Hello, world!\nThis is a test.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This will output:

```
<h1>Hello, world!</h1><p>This is a test.</p>
```

Note that this function assumes that the markdown text is well-formed and does not contain any special characters or HTML tags. If you need to handle more complex markdown syntax, you may need to use a more robust parser or library to convert the markdown text.

